Subject: Resolve a type named through a using-declaration

`using std::string;` followed by `void f(string a)` binds `a` as
`std::basic_string<...>` - and, where that type is opaque, as a blob of bytes -
rather than as the `std::string` the parameter was written with, which is what
the same function written `void f(std::string a)` binds it as.

The declaration is lost one level below bindgen. clang has a `UsingType` sugar
node for a name introduced by a using-declaration, and libclang has no `CXType`
for it: `clang_getTypeDeclaration` answers `CXCursor_NoDeclFound`, and
`Type::canonical_declaration`'s one rescue for a declaration-less type is
template-specific, so the type falls through to its canonical type and is bound
as whatever that is. Nothing is wrong with the canonical type except that it is
not what the header says: an alias replaced by
`<div rustbindgen replaces="...">`, an alias whose target is opaque, and an
alias asked for by name in an allowlist all bind as something else.

What does survive is the name clang prints for the type. Namespace-scope
using-declarations are visited during parsing, so record the type declarations
each of them introduces, keyed on the name clang prints for each, and resolve a
declaration-less type through the record. A printed name is not a proof, so a
candidate is a match only where its canonical type is the type's as well.

The name is compared as clang prints it, because that is the one description of
a name which is the same across libclang versions, and they do not agree here.
Through clang 21 such a type arrives as `CXType_Elaborated` over an unexposed
node, and that node carries the *qualified* name of the declaration and none of
the qualifiers written in front of it: `std::string` for a parameter written
`string`. From clang 22 it arrives unexposed with no such node, spelling the
name as it was written and the qualifiers with it: `const fx_BU`. Asking
`clang_Type_getNamedType` where it answers, and taking the type's own qualifiers
off the name, covers both. A `const T&` parameter is what shows why both halves
are needed: from clang 22 its pointee would otherwise look for a name nothing
was recorded under.

Since the name which leaves that is not always qualified, it is the type behind
it that settles a candidate, and by its declaration rather than by a second
comparison of printed names: two canonical types can print alike, as two classes
local to different functions both print as `X`, and a declaration also carries
none of the qualifiers a parameter may add. Only a name standing for a type C++
declared is resolved at all. One whose canonical type has no declaration - a
builtin, a pointer - is left alone, which buys nothing anyway, since bindgen
already generates what it stands for. A name which two candidates answer to with
one declaration resolves to neither: they are two names for the same thing and
nothing here says which was written, so choosing would be a guess.

Two details of what libclang exposes. `clang_getNumOverloadedDecls` answers zero
asked on the using-declaration cursor; the declarations it introduces hang off
its `CXCursor_OverloadedDeclRef` child. And the record is made at the top of
`Item::parse` because a using-declaration never reaches the `match cursor.kind()`
at the end of it: `cursor.definition()` answers with that same
`CXCursor_OverloadedDeclRef`, which is not the cursor itself, so the type
handling above the match returns an `UnresolvedTypeRef` for the declaration
first. The arm listing `CXCursor_UsingDeclaration` among the kinds deliberately
not handled is therefore already dead, and is left as it is.

A name is not resolved to the declaration whose own definition is being parsed.
Such a type would stand for something which stands, in the end, for the type
itself, and nothing downstream is obliged to cope with a type graph that comes
back round. The ordinary resolution path cannot produce one, because it is
reached from the declaration and bindgen stops it there; resolution by name
arrives from the use site, with no such stop, so it asks.

Only type declarations are recorded, and a template is not one: a
using-declaration naming a function, a variable or a template introduces
nothing a type can be written as, since a use of a template carries template
arguments and has a declaration of its own. Recording a template would only
offer `builtin_or_resolved_ty` a template-like declaration to instantiate -
recursively, through every argument - which is not a thing this should be able
to start.
